  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 327 AM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nFrom the end of the weekend through early next week, a closed mid-\nlevel low will remain parked over the eastern Gulf. Warm and moist \nair wrapping around the low from the Atlantic will pool across the \narea, supporting scattered showers and storms each day. High \ntemperatures will remain in the 90s, combining with dewpoints in the \n70s to drive heat index values into the upper 90s to low 100s.\n\nConfidence in the forecast drops from mid to late next week, but \nmodel guidance has started to gradually trend towards a cooler \n'wedge' pattern. This setup would involve the mid-level low being \nabsorbed by a trough to the north, with strong surface high pressure \nsliding over the Northeast US. Low-level flow would turn out of the \nnortheast, bringing cooler air in along the eastern side of the \nAppalachians. The latest guidance has highs falling to mid 80s \nWednesday, and upper 70s to low 80s by Friday, with only slight rain \nchances. It is worth noting there are quite a few members of \nguidance that either keep the mid-level low in place over the \neastern Gulf, or build a ridge back over the Southeast. Either of \nthese scenarios would lead to continued warm temperatures and daily \nshowers and storms. Over the next few days, this picture will become \nmuch clearer.",
